{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor path in [\n    '/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/15/prompt.md',\n    '/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/15/des.md',\n    '/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/15/1.py',\n]:\n    text = Path(path).read_text()\n    print(f'FILE: {path}')\n    lines = text.splitlines()\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines[:260], 1):\n        print(f'{i:4}: {line}')\n    print('FILEEND')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"FILE: /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/15/prompt.md\n   1: 你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n   2: \n   3: 每个参考题描述包含：\n   4: - `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n   5: - `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n   6: - `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n   7: - `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n   8: \n   9: 参考题目如下：\n  10: <reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84ba50d3\" lines=\"36-54\">\n  11: <description>\n  12: Drop the blue sprites downward relative to the red divider, with vertical sprites passing through to the bottom.\n  13: \n  14: Input structure:\n  15: - The background is the dominant color.\n  16: - One non-background color forms a single full-width horizontal divider row.\n  17: - The other non-background color forms 4-connected sprites above the divider.\n  18: \n  19: Rule:\n  20: - Clear the original sprite pixels.\n  21: - A one-column sprite falls to the bottom of its column and deletes the divider\n  22:   pixel it crossed.\n  23: - Any wider sprite falls until its unique widest horizontal row is immediately\n  24:   above the divider row; if the moved sprite overlaps the divider, it covers it.\n  25: \n  26: Key insight:\n  27: The red row is not a generic floor: only one-column sprites pass through to the\n  28: bottom, while wider sprites are registered by their widest horizontal segment.\n  29: </description>\n  30: </reference_puzzle>\n  31: \n  32: <reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1b8318e3\" lines=\"86-105\">\n  33: <description>\n  34: Move each isolated colored pixel into the one-cell frame around a grey 2x2 block.\n  35: \n  36: Input structure:\n  37: - Background is the dominant color.\n  38: - Grey foreground objects are solid 2x2 anchor blocks.\n  39: - Every other non-background, non-grey cell is a colored singleton source.\n  40: \n  41: Rule:\n  42: - Keep the grey anchors fixed and clear all colored singleton source pixels.\n  43: - For each singleton and each anchor, the normal destination is the cell reached by\n  44:   clamping the pixel into the anchor's one-cell outer frame.\n  45: - If a diagonally outside singleton has an exact 8-direction sightline into an anchor,\n  46:   also allow the cell just before the first grey pixel on that ray.\n  47: - Choose distinct destinations that minimize total king-move distance; at equal\n  48:   distance prefer exact sightlines, then the shorter Manhattan movement.\n  49: \n  50: Key insight: The grey 2x2 blocks define magnetic frames; conflicts are resolved\n  51: structurally by nearest-frame assignment rather than by color identity.\n  52: </description>\n  53: </reference_puzzle>\n  54: \n  55: <reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84551f4c\" lines=\"42-53\">\n  56: <description>\n  57: Rotate each blue-started chain of bottom-aligned vertical bars into horizontal bars resting on the same baseline.\n  58: Input structure\n  59: - The grid has a background and bottom-aligned, one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n  60: - Blue bars are markers; red bars are body bars that may be chained after a marker.\n  61: Rule\n  62: - Starting at each blue bar, repeatedly include the next red bar whose column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height.\n  63: - Clear every included vertical bar, then redraw each included bar horizontally to the right from its original bottom cell, with length equal to its vertical height.\n  64: - Red bars that are not reached from a blue marker remain unchanged.\n  65: Key insight\n  66: - A vertical bar's height is also the width it occupies after rotation, so the chain follows immediate horizontal contact after each rotated bar.\n  67: </description>\n  68: </reference_puzzle>\n  69: \n  70: <reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"41ace6b5\" lines=\"7-26\">\n  71: <description>\n  72: Normalize vertical two-color bars between paired marker columns around the marker rows.\n  73: \n  74: Input structure\n  75: - The background is the dominant color.\n  76: - A row of red marker pixels sits directly above a row of gray marker pixels\n  77:   in the same alternating columns.\n  78: - The columns between adjacent markers contain vertical bars made from a\n  79:   contiguous teal run above a contiguous blue run.\n  80: \n  81: Rule\n  82: - Keep the marker pixels and all non-bar background cells fixed.\n  83: - Let h be the maximum teal-run height among the bar columns; paint teal in\n  84:   every bar column for h rows ending at the red marker row.\n  85: - In each bar column, move its blue run to start at the gray marker row and\n  86:   fill all lower cells in that column with maroon.\n  87: \n  88: Key insight\n  89: - The marker rows are a baseline: teal is normalized into a common upper\n  90:   band, while each blue run keeps its own height below the baseline.\n  91: </description>\n  92: </reference_puzzle>\n  93: \n  94: <reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"54dc2872\" lines=\"52-69\">\n  95: <description>\n  96: Move each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.\n  97: \n  98: Input structure\n  99: - The background is the dominant color.\n 100: - Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls.\n 101: - Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker\n 102:   pixel of a different color.\n 103: \n 104: Rule\n 105: - If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,\n 106:   translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel.\n 107: - Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,\n 108:   then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.\n 109: \n 110: Key insight\n 111: - The marker color pairs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend\n 112:   gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination.\n 113: </description>\n 114: </reference_puzzle>\n 115: \n 116: <reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"edcc2ff0\" lines=\"47-63\">\n 117: <description>\n 118: Keep only the lower-panel colors named by the top markers and expand each marker into an object-count bar.\n 119: \n 120: Input structure\n 121: A black instruction strip sits above a non-black rectangular scene panel.  The strip contains one-cell markers\n 122: in the first column, and each marker color is a candidate object color in the lower panel.  The lower panel has\n 123: a unique dominant background color and scattered 4-connected foreground objects.\n 124: \n 125: Rule\n 126: In the lower panel, preserve objects whose colors appear as top markers and recolor every other foreground\n 127: object to the panel background.  In the instruction strip, replace each marker by a horizontal run from column 0\n 128: whose length is the number of 4-connected lower-panel objects of that marker color; erase markers with count 0.\n 129: \n 130: Key insight\n 131: The top strip is a color filter plus a compact count summary: marker colors select which lower-panel objects\n 132: survive, and their bars report the number of surviving objects by color.\n 133: </description>\n 134: </reference_puzzle>\n 135: \n 136: <reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88e364bc\" lines=\"102-115\">\n 137: <description>\n 138: Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n 139: \n 140: Input structure:\n 141: - bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n 142: - Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n 143: \n 144: Rule:\n 145: - For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n 146: - The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n 147: - All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n 148: \n 149: Key insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\n 150: </description>\n 151: </reference_puzzle>\n 152: \n 153: <reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"291dc1e1\" lines=\"7-22\">\n 154: <description>\n 155: Reorder and stack rectangles by proximity to two colored edge lines.\n 156: \n 157: Input structure:\n 158: - bg = most common color.\n 159: - Two edge lines (h + v) meet at a corner marked by a unique color.\n 160: - \"Blue\" line is color 1 if present, else the longer line; other is \"red.\"\n 161: - Solid rectangles in the interior.\n 162: \n 163: Rule:\n 164: - Sort rectangles by (distance to blue edge, distance to red edge).\n 165: - If the blue edge is vertical, rotate each rectangle 90° CCW.\n 166: - Stack top-to-bottom, centered horizontally.\n 167: \n 168: Key insight: Edge-line colors encode a coordinate system; proximity to each axis determines stacking order and rotation.\n 169: </description>\n 170: </reference_puzzle>\n 171: \n 172: <reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e4941b18\" lines=\"8-22\">\n 173: <description>\n 174: Move red into teal's top marker position, then move teal to the bottom outside corner of the grey rectangle.\n 175: \n 176: Input structure\n 177: - The dominant background surrounds one filled rectangular non-background block.\n 178: - There is exactly one red marker and one teal marker, both in the row immediately above the rectangle.\n 179: - Both markers are horizontally aligned over columns inside the rectangle.\n 180: \n 181: Rule\n 182: - Clear the original red marker.\n 183: - Recolor the original teal marker cell red.\n 184: - Place the teal marker beside the rectangle's bottom row, on the side indicated by the horizontal direction from red to teal.\n 185: \n 186: Key insight\n 187: - The red-to-teal horizontal order chooses the rectangle side where teal exits at the bottom.\n 188: </description>\n 189: </reference_puzzle>\n 190: \n 191: <reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"17b866bd\" lines=\"49-67\">\n 192: <description>\n 193: Move each marker from a lattice corner gap into the adjacent enclosed 12-pixel cavity.\n 194: \n 195: Input structure:\n 196: - The grid is a repeated teal lattice around black 12-pixel cavities.\n 197: - A marker is a non-black pixel placed at the northwest corner gap of one\n 198:   complete 5x5 lattice tile; the marker may itself be teal.\n 199: \n 200: Rule:\n 201: - For every marked northwest corner gap, clear that marker to black.\n 202: - Fill the 12 black pixels in the tile immediately down-right of the marker\n 203:   with the marker color.\n 204: - Leave the rest of the lattice unchanged.\n 205: \n 206: Key insight:\n 207: - The apparent isolated markers are control pixels for the adjacent cavity;\n 208:   teal markers are recognized as extra teal pixels occupying a gap that the\n 209:   periodic lattice template says should be black.\n 210: </description>\n 211: </reference_puzzle>\n 212: \n 213: <reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b3084d4\" lines=\"253-266\">\n 214: <description>\n 215: Assemble colored rectangular sprites into a single tiled rectangle, anchored by a marker pixel.\n 216: \n 217: Input structure:\n 218: - bg = most common color. Several solid-colored rectangular sprites scattered on the grid.\n 219: - One sprite has a unique single-pixel marker (different color) at one corner — this is the anchor.\n 220: \n 221: Rule:\n 222: - Identify the anchor sprite (contains the marker pixel) and the remaining pieces.\n 223: - Rotate the anchor so the marker pixel lands at position (0,0) of the output.\n 224: - Pack all remaining pieces (allowing 90° rotations) to tile a complete rectangle with no gaps.\n 225: \n 226: Key insight: The lone marker pixel picks the anchor and fixes the output orientation — it must sit at the top-left corner.\n 227: </description>\n 228: </reference_puzzle>\n 229: \n 230: <reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8f215267\" lines=\"7-21\">\n 231: <description>\n 232: Count same-color patterns per frame and represent the tally as dots inside.\n 233: \n 234: Input structure:\n 235: - bg = most common color.\n 236: - Hollow rectangular frames in various colors.\n 237: - Small monochrome pattern sprites scattered outside frames.\n 238: \n 239: Rule:\n 240: - Remove all small patterns from the grid.\n 241: - For each frame of color C, count how many patterns share color C.\n 242: - Draw that many dots along the frame's center row, spaced by 2, starting from the right.\n 243: \n 244: Key insight: Frames act as tally displays — scattered same-color patterns are \"absorbed\" and their count is shown as interior dots.\n 245: </description>\n 246: </reference_puzzle>\n 247: \n 248: <reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5ecac7f7\" lines=\"23-36\">\n 249: <description>\n 250: Combine the left, middle, and right ordered objects from three separated panels.\n 251: \n 252: Input structure\n 253: - The grid is three HxH panels separated by one-cell vertical separator bars.\n 254: - The background is the dominant color, and each panel contains exactly three same-color 4-connected foreground objects.\n 255: \n 256: Rule\n 257: - Build a new HxH background canvas.\n 258: - From the left panel copy its leftmost object, from the middle panel copy its middle object, and from the right panel copy its rightmost object.\n 259: \n 260: Key insight\nFILEEND\nFILE: /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/15/des.md\n   1: <new_puzzle>\n   2: - `description`：在分层网格中，每层的前景图案充当下一层的遮罩模版，逐层级联过滤后输出末层的残留结果。\n   3: - `Input structure`：\n   4:   - 网格被等宽的 maroon 垂直分隔线分为三个并排面板（从左到右称为第1层、第2层、第3层）。\n   5:   - 每个面板大小完全相同，背景为 black。\n   6:   - 第1层包含若干由某种颜色（如 red）绘制的4-连通前景区域。\n   7:   - 第2层包含若干由另一种颜色（如 green）绘制的4-连通前景区域。\n   8:   - 第3层包含若干由第三种颜色（如 yellow）绘制的4-连通前景区域。\n   9:   - 三层中的前景图案形状各异，彼此有部分位置重叠、部分不重叠。\n  10: - `Rule`：\n  11:   - 将第1层前景像素的位置集合作为遮罩，应用到第2层：仅保留第2层中与第1层前景位置重合的像素，第2层其余前景像素清除为 black。\n  12:   - 将经过上一步过滤后第2层残留前景像素的位置集合作为新遮罩，应用到第3层：仅保留第3层中与该新遮罩位置重合的像素，第3层其余前景像素清除为 black。\n  13:   - 输出为单个面板大小的网格，内容为最终过滤后的第3层图案，保留第3层原有颜色，背景为 black。\n  14: - `Key insight`：三层面板构成级联过滤管道——第1层是第2层的筛子，第2层被筛后又变成第3层的筛子。解题者必须识别逐层遮罩传递关系，而非简单的三层叠加或交集运算。\n  15: </new_puzzle>\n  16: \n  17: <new_puzzle>\n  18: - `description`：将散落的彩色碎片按同色配对、互补拼合成完整矩形，再按矩形面积从小到大垂直堆叠输出。\n  19: - `Input structure`：\n  20:   - 背景为 azure。\n  21:   - 网格中散布偶数个4-连通彩色碎片。碎片两两同色，不同对颜色不同。\n  22:   - 同色的两个碎片形状互补：将二者的边界框对齐后，两个碎片的像素并集恰好填满整个边界框，无重叠、无空隙。\n  23:   - 每个碎片的边界框尺寸与其配对碎片相同。\n  24: - `Rule`：\n  25:   - 找到所有同色碎片对。\n  26:   - 对每对碎片，将两者边界框对齐叠加，得到一个完整的实心矩形，颜色为该对碎片的颜色。\n  27:   - 计算每个矩形的面积（宽 x 高）。\n  28:   - 构建输出网格：将所有矩形按面积从小到大、从上到下紧密排列，每个矩形左对齐，矩形之间无间距。输出宽度等于最宽矩形的宽度，高度等于所有矩形高度之和，空余位置填充 azure。\n  29: - `Key insight`：同色碎片不是冗余——它们是互补拼图碎片。解题者需先发现互补配对关系、将碎片在脑中合并为矩形，再根据面积排序输出，这是两步独立的非平凡推理。\n  30: </new_puzzle>\n  31: \n  32: <new_puzzle>\n  33: - `description`：识别每个封闭房间\"门\"的朝向，将房间内物体在垂直于门朝向上的尺寸转化为从门口射出的等长射线。\n  34: - `Input structure`：\n  35:   - 背景为 black。\n  36:   - 网格中有若干由 blue 像素围成的封闭矩形房间（边框宽度1像素）。房间之间互不重叠。\n  37:   - 每个房间的边框上恰好有一个1像素缺口（\"门\"）。\n  38:   - 每个房间内部有一个小型实心矩形物体（1-3像素宽或高），颜色为非 blue、非 black 的某种颜色，各房间物体颜色互不相同。\n  39: - `Rule`：\n  40:   - 确定门所在边：上边框缺口 → 朝上，下边框 → 朝下，左边框 → 朝左，右边框 → 朝右。\n  41:   - 计算物体在**垂直于门朝向**方向上的像素跨度 L。例如门朝上或朝下时，L = 物体的高度（行数）；门朝左或朝右时，L = 物体的宽度（列数）。\n  42:   - 清除房间内的物体像素。\n  43:   - 从门缺口紧邻外侧开始，沿门朝向绘制一条长度为 L、宽度为1像素的直线，颜色为该物体颜色。\n  44:   - 房间边框（含缺口）保持不变。\n  45: - `Key insight`：门的方位编码弹射方向，而物体在正交方向上的尺寸编码射线长度——两个信息分别来自房间结构和物体形状，需要组合推理才能确定输出。\n  46: </new_puzzle>\n  47: \n  48: <new_puzzle>\n  49: - `description`：根据围栏内部线段的方向和围栏缺口的位置，将围栏外的同色游离像素射入围栏内部进行堆叠填充。\n  50: - `Input structure`：\n  51:   - 背景为 black。\n  52:   - 网格中有若干不同颜色的空心矩形围栏（边框宽度1像素）。\n  53:   - 每个围栏内部有一条由某种非围栏色、非背景色组成的短直线段（水平或垂直，长度>=2像素）。\n  54:   - 每个围栏的某一侧边框上有一个1像素缺口；缺口所在的边与内部线段方向垂直（即水平线段对应上或下缺口，垂直线段对应左或右缺口）。\n  55:   - 围栏外散布若干游离单像素，颜色与某个围栏内线段颜色相同。\n  56: - `Rule`：\n  57:   - 内部线段的方向确定填充轴：水平线段 → 水平方向堆叠，垂直线段 → 垂直方向堆叠。\n  58:   - 缺口所在边确定填充朝向：缺口在上 → 从上向下，缺口在下 → 从下向上，缺口在左 → 从左向右，缺口在右 → 从右向左。\n  59:   - 统计与该围栏内线段同色的外部游离像素数量 N，清除这些游离像素。\n  60:   - 在围栏内部，从缺口对面内壁开始，沿填充朝向依次放置 N 个该颜色像素（逐格填充），不覆盖已有的线段像素，跳过线段占据的位置继续放置。\n  61: - `Key insight`：围栏内的线段方向编码填充轴，缺口位置编码填充朝向，外部同色像素数量编码填充深度。三个独立线索需要从不同结构元素中分别提取并组合。\n  62: </new_puzzle>\n  63: \n  64: <new_puzzle>\n  65: - `description`：彩色箭头通过指向方向形成传递链，链首的颜色被传递到链末端并以角标形式标记。\n  66: - `Input structure`：\n  67:   - 背景为 black。\n  68:   - 网格中有若干\"箭头\"对象，每个箭头颜色各不相同，由5个像素绘制而成。\n  69:   - 箭头结构：以中心像素为核心，沿指向方向延伸2个像素（中心 + 前方1格），同时在中心像素的两侧（垂直于指向方向）各延伸1个像素作为侧翼。共5像素组成T形，其中T的竖笔朝向即为箭头指向。\n  70:   - 每个箭头的指向为上、下、左、右之一。\n  71: - `Rule`：\n  72:   - 对每个箭头 A，沿其指向方向做射线搜索，找到射线上命中的第一个其他箭头 B（射线碰到 B 的任意像素即可），形成有向边 A → B。\n  73:   - 沿有向边构建链：找到链首（不被任何箭头指向的箭头），依次跟随有向边直到链末端（其射线上无其他箭头）。\n  74:   - 在链末端箭头的边界框四角外扩1格的位置（即边界框外扩后矩形的四个顶角位置），各放置1个像素，颜色为链首箭头的颜色。\n  75:   - 链首和链中间的箭头保持不变。\n  76: - `Key insight`：箭头的T形缺口方向编码指向，指向决定链接关系。解题者需要三步推理：先从形状解读指向方向，再沿射线构建传递链，最后将链首颜色信息传递标记到链末端。\n  77: </new_puzzle>\n  78: \n  79: <new_puzzle>\n  80: - `description`：每个彩色种子从管道末端同步扩散着色，两色前锋相遇处保留灰色边界，最终整条管道网络被分段着色。\n  81: - `Input structure`：\n  82:   - 背景为 black。\n  83:   - 网格中有一个由 grey 像素构成的连通管道网络，管道宽度恒为1像素，由若干水平和垂直路径组成，路径在交叉点相交。\n  84:   - 管道网络的若干死端（度为1的末梢像素）旁各有一个不同颜色的单像素\"种子\"（4-相邻于该末梢灰色像素），种子颜色互不相同且不含 grey 和 black。\n  85:   - 并非所有死端都有种子。\n  86: - `Rule`：\n  87:   - 所有种子同时从各自相邻的管道末梢开始，沿灰色管道向内扩散，每步将1个相邻的 grey 像素重新着色为自身颜色。\n  88:   - 扩散同步进行（所有种子同时各走一步）。\n  89:   - 当两个不同颜色的扩散前锋同时到达同一个 grey 像素时，该像素保持 grey 不变，成为边界。\n  90:   - 扩散完成后，清除所有种子像素（变为 black）。\n  91:   - 输出网格中管道被分段着色，各段之间以 grey 像素为界。\n  92: - `Key insight`：管道着色由同步洪水填充决定，而非距离公式。解题者需在脑中模拟同步扩散过程——沿管道路径数格子即可判断每段归属，分界点自然出现在两个种子沿管道等距之处。\n  93: </new_puzzle>\n  94: \n  95: <new_puzzle>\n  96: - `description`：中心十字将网格分为四象限，十字四端的颜色标记编码几何变换操作，每个象限的对象受其对应行端和列端标记的复合变换。\n  97: - `Input structure`：\n  98:   - 网格尺寸为奇数 x 奇数，背景为 black。\n  99:   - 网格正中心行和中心列被 red 像素填满，形成十字分隔线，将网格分为四个象限（左上、右上、左下、右下）。\n 100:   - 十字的上端（中心列第0行）、下端（中心列最末行）、左端（中心行第0列）、右端（中心行最末列）各有1个非 red 非 black 的标记像素替换了原本的 red 像素。\n 101:   - 四个端点标记颜色从 {green, yellow, blue, orange} 中各取一个，互不重复。颜色含义：green = 保持原样，yellow = 水平翻转，blue = 垂直翻转，orange = 旋转180度。\n 102:   - 每个象限中有一个小型（不超过 3x3）grey 4-连通前景对象。\n 103: - `Rule`：\n 104:   - 上端标记控制上方两个象限（左上、右上），下端标记控制下方两个象限（左下、右下）——称为\"行操作\"。\n 105:   - 左端标记控制左侧两个象限（左上、左下），右端标记控制右侧两个象限（右上、右下）——称为\"列操作\"。\n 106:   - 每个象限受行操作和列操作的复合作用：先执行列操作，再执行行操作。变换以对象自身中心为锚点原地执行。\n 107:   - 例如左上象限受上端标记的行操作和左端标记的列操作复合影响。\n 108: - `Key insight`：十字末端的四个颜色形成一个行列交叉索引的变换矩阵。解题者需要先破译颜色与操作的对应关系，再理解行列复合机制，两步推理共同决定每个象限的变换结果。\n 109: </new_puzzle>\n 110: \n 111: <new_puzzle>\n 112: - `description`：根据框架内嵌小方块的颜色查找对应的外部色带图例，用图例中的条纹方向重新填充框架内部。\n 113: - `Input structure`：\n 114:   - 背景为 black。\n 115:   - 网格中有若干大型空心矩形框架（边框颜色各不相同，宽度1像素），框架内部为 black 背景，但每个框架内部的左上角有一个 2x2 的实心小方块，颜色为非框架色的某种颜色。\n 116:   - 网格边缘（不与任何框架重叠的区域）有若干\"色带图例\"：每个图例是一个 3x3 的方格，其边框像素的颜色为某种颜色 C（与某个框架内小方块的颜色 C 一致），内部由 C 色像素组成条纹图案——要么是1像素宽的水平条纹（即中间行填满 C，上下行为 black），要么是1像素宽的垂直条纹（即中间列填满 C，左右列为 black）。\n 117: - `Rule`：\n 118:   - 对每个框架，读取其内部左上角 2x2 小方块的颜色 C。\n 119:   - 在网格边缘找到边框颜色为 C 的 3x3 图例，判断图例内部条纹方向（水平或垂直）。\n 120:   - 用颜色 C 按该条纹方向填充框架的全部内部空间：水平条纹则在框架内部每隔一行填一行 C（从内部第一行开始，奇数行填充、偶数行保留 black）；垂直条纹则每隔一列填一列 C（从内部第一列开始，奇数列填充、偶数列保留 black）。\n 121:   - 移除原来的 2x2 小方块（被条纹覆盖或清除）。\n 122:   - 图例保持不变。\n 123: - `Key insight`：框架内的小方块是颜色索引键，用于关联外部图例；图例的条纹方向决定框架的填充模式。解题者需要先建立\"小方块颜色 → 图例\"的映射，再从图例中提取方向信息，两步查找缺一不可。\n 124: </new_puzzle>\nFILEEND\nFILE: /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/15/1.py\n   1: import numpy as np\n   2: \n   3: \n   4: BG = 0\n   5: SEP = 9\n   6: COLOR_POOL = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8)\n   7: \n   8: A_BIT = 1\n   9: B_BIT = 2\n  10: C_BIT = 4\n  11: \n  12: DIRS4 = ((-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1))\n  13: GENERATION_ATTEMPTS = 400\n  14: BLOB_ATTEMPTS = 60\n  15: \n  16: \n  17: def _dominant_color(grid: np.ndarray) -> int:\n  18:     values, counts = np.unique(grid, return_counts=True)\n  19:     return int(values[np.argmax(counts)])\n  20: \n  21: \n  22: def _find_panels(grid: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> tuple[list[np.ndarray], int]:\n  23:     h, w = grid.shape\n  24:     sep_cols = [c for c in range(w) if int(grid[0, c]) != bg and np.all(grid[:, c] == grid[0, c])]\n  25:     matches: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n  26:     for i, c1 in enumerate(sep_cols):\n  27:         for c2 in sep_cols[i + 1 :]:\n  28:             left_w = c1\n  29:             mid_w = c2 - c1 - 1\n  30:             right_w = w - c2 - 1\n  31:             if left_w <= 0 or left_w != mid_w or left_w != right_w:\n  32:                 continue\n  33:             if int(grid[0, c1]) != int(grid[0, c2]):\n  34:                 continue\n  35:             matches.append((c1, c2))\n  36:     assert len(matches) == 1, \"expected exactly two equal-width separator-delimited cuts\"\n  37:     c1, c2 = matches[0]\n  38:     panels = [grid[:, :c1], grid[:, c1 + 1 : c2], grid[:, c2 + 1 :]]\n  39:     assert panels[0].shape == panels[1].shape == panels[2].shape\n  40:     return panels, int(grid[0, c1])\n  41: \n  42: \n  43: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n  44:     \"\"\"\n  45:     Cascade three separator-delimited masks so each layer filters the next one.\n  46: \n  47:     Input structure:\n  48:     - The grid contains three equal-size panels arranged left to right.\n  49:     - Two full-height non-background separator columns split the panels.\n  50:     - Each panel has a black background and a single foreground color forming one or more 4-connected regions.\n  51: \n  52:     Rule:\n  53:     - Use the foreground positions of the first panel as a mask on the second panel and erase every second-panel foreground pixel outside that mask.\n  54:     - Use the surviving second-panel foreground positions as a new mask on the third panel and erase every third-panel foreground pixel outside that new mask.\n  55:     - Return a single-panel grid containing only the remaining third-panel pixels in their original color on a black background.\n  56: \n  57:     Key insight:\n  58:     - The three panels are not combined symmetrically; they form a left-to-right filtering pipeline where each residue becomes the next mask.\n  59:     \"\"\"\n  60:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n  61:     bg = _dominant_color(g)\n  62:     panels, sep_color = _find_panels(g, bg)\n  63:     assert sep_color != bg\n  64: \n  65:     first, second, third = panels\n  66:     second_after_first = np.where(first != bg, second, bg)\n  67:     out = np.full(third.shape, bg, dtype=int)\n  68:     keep = (second_after_first != bg) & (third != bg)\n  69:     out[keep] = third[keep]\n  70:     return out\n  71: \n  72: \n  73: def _dilate4(mask: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n  74:     out = mask.copy()\n  75:     out[1:, :] |= mask[:-1, :]\n  76:     out[:-1, :] |= mask[1:, :]\n  77:     out[:, 1:] |= mask[:, :-1]\n  78:     out[:, :-1] |= mask[:, 1:]\n  79:     return out\n  80: \n  81: \n  82: def _blob_neighbors(cell: tuple[int, int], h: int, w: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n  83:     r, c = cell\n  84:     out: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n  85:     for dr, dc in DIRS4:\n  86:         nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n  87:         if 0 <= nr < h and 0 <= nc < w:\n  88:             out.append((nr, nc))\n  89:     return out\n  90: \n  91: \n  92: def _sample_blob(\n  93:     rng: np.random.Generator,\n  94:     shape: tuple[int, int],\n  95:     occupied: np.ndarray,\n  96:     min_size: int,\n  97:     max_size: int,\n  98: ) -> np.ndarray | None:\n  99:     h, w = shape\n 100:     guard = _dilate4(occupied)\n 101:     free = np.argwhere(~guard)\n 102:     if free.size == 0:\n 103:         return None\n 104: \n 105:     min_size = max(1, min_size)\n 106:     max_size = max(min_size, max_size)\n 107:     for _ in range(BLOB_ATTEMPTS):\n 108:         seed_r, seed_c = free[int(rng.integers(len(free)))]\n 109:         blob = {(int(seed_r), int(seed_c))}\n 110:         frontier = set(_blob_neighbors((int(seed_r), int(seed_c)), h, w))\n 111:         target = int(rng.integers(min_size, max_size + 1))\n 112: \n 113:         while frontier and len(blob) < target:\n 114:             choice = list(frontier)[int(rng.integers(len(frontier)))]\n 115:             frontier.remove(choice)\n 116:             if guard[choice]:\n 117:                 continue\n 118:             blob.add(choice)\n 119:             for nxt in _blob_neighbors(choice, h, w):\n 120:                 if nxt not in blob:\n 121:                     frontier.add(nxt)\n 122:             if len(blob) >= min_size and rng.random() < 0.18:\n 123:                 break\n 124: \n 125:         if len(blob) >= min_size:\n 126:             coords = np.array(sorted(blob), dtype=int)\n 127:             return coords\n 128:     return None\n 129: \n 130: \n 131: def _combo_size_bounds(combo: int, panel_area: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n 132:     if combo in (A_BIT | B_BIT | C_BIT, A_BIT | B_BIT):\n 133:         high = 6 if panel_area < 64 else 7\n 134:         return 3, high\n 135:     high = 4 if panel_area < 64 else 5\n 136:     return 2, high\n 137: \n 138: \n 139: def _sample_role_grid(rng: np.random.Generator, h: int, w: int) -> np.ndarray | None:\n 140:     roles = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=int)\n 141:     mandatory = [\n 142:         A_BIT | B_BIT | C_BIT,\n 143:         A_BIT | B_BIT,\n 144:         int(rng.choice((A_BIT, A_BIT | C_BIT))),\n 145:         int(rng.choice((B_BIT, B_BIT | C_BIT))),\n 146:         int(rng.choice((C_BIT, A_BIT | C_BIT, B_BIT | C_BIT))),\n 147:     ]\n 148:     extra_cap = 1 + int(h * w >= 56) + int(h * w >= 72)\n 149:     optional = list(\n 150:         map(\n 151:             int,\n 152:             rng.choice(\n 153:                 (A_BIT, B_BIT, C_BIT, A_BIT | B_BIT, A_BIT | C_BIT, B_BIT | C_BIT, A_BIT | B_BIT | C_BIT),\n 154:                 size=int(rng.integers(0, extra_cap + 1)),\n 155:                 replace=True,\n 156:             ),\n 157:         )\n 158:     )\n 159:     combos = mandatory + optional\n 160:     rng.shuffle(combos)\n 161: \n 162:     for combo in combos:\n 163:         low, high = _combo_size_bounds(combo, h * w)\n 164:         blob = _sample_blob(rng, (h, w), roles != 0, low, high)\n 165:         if blob is None:\n 166:             return None\n 167:         roles[blob[:, 0], blob[:, 1]] = combo\n 168:     return roles\n 169: \n 170: \n 171: def _masks_from_roles(roles: np.ndarray) -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n 172:     return (\n 173:         (roles & A_BIT) != 0,\n 174:         (roles & B_BIT) != 0,\n 175:         (roles & C_BIT) != 0,\n 176:     )\n 177: \n 178: \n 179: def _valid_masks(masks: tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray, np.ndarray]) -> bool:\n 180:     m1, m2, m3 = masks\n 181:     panel_area = int(m1.size)\n 182:     areas = [int(m.sum()) for m in masks]\n 183:     if min(areas) == 0 or max(areas) > int(np.ceil(panel_area * 0.55)):\n 184:         return False\n 185: \n 186:     inter12 = m1 & m2\n 187:     final = inter12 & m3\n 188:     if int(inter12.sum()) < 4 or int(final.sum()) < 2:\n 189:         return False\n 190:     if not (m1 & ~m2).any():\n 191:         return False\n 192:     if not (m2 & ~m1).any():\n 193:         return False\n 194:     if not (inter12 & ~m3).any():\n 195:         return False\n 196:     if not (m3 & ~inter12).any():\n 197:         return False\n 198:     return True\n 199: \n 200: \n 201: def _build_grid(\n 202:     masks: tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray, np.ndarray],\n 203:     colors: tuple[int, int, int],\n 204: ) -> np.ndarray:\n 205:     h, w = masks[0].shape\n 206:     grid = np.full((h, 3 * w + 2), BG, dtype=int)\n 207:     grid[:, w] = SEP\n 208:     grid[:, 2 * w + 1] = SEP\n 209:     starts = (0, w + 1, 2 * w + 2)\n 210:     for start, mask, color in zip(starts, masks, colors):\n 211:         panel = grid[:, start : start + w]\n 212:         panel[mask] = int(color)\n 213:     return grid\n 214: \n 215: \n 216: def _gen_attempt(rng: np.random.Generator) -> np.ndarray | None:\n 217:     h = int(rng.integers(6, 13))\n 218:     w = int(rng.integers(6, 10))\n 219:     if 3 * w + 2 > 30:\n 220:         return None\n 221: \n 222:     roles = _sample_role_grid(rng, h, w)\n 223:     if roles is None or len(set(map(int, roles[roles != 0].ravel()))) < 4:\n 224:         return None\n 225: \n 226:     masks = _masks_from_roles(roles)\n 227:     if not _valid_masks(masks):\n 228:         return None\n 229: \n 230:     colors = tuple(map(int, rng.choice(COLOR_POOL, size=3, replace=False)))\n 231:     grid = _build_grid(masks, colors)\n 232: \n 233:     expected = np.full((h, w), BG, dtype=int)\n 234:     expected[masks[0] & masks[1] & masks[2]] = colors[2]\n 235:     try:\n 236:         actual = main(grid)\n 237:     except AssertionError:\n 238:         return None\n 239:     if not np.array_equal(actual, expected):\n 240:         return None\n 241:     return grid\n 242: \n 243: \n 244: def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n 245:     rng = np.random.default_rng()\n 246:     for _ in range(GENERATION_ATTEMPTS):\n 247:         grid = _gen_attempt(rng)\n 248:         if grid is not None:\n 249:             return grid\n 250:     raise RuntimeError(f\"failed to generate valid input after {GENERATION_ATTEMPTS} attempts\")\nFILEEND\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
